WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.709
Film Courage: In a pitch meeting, what are
the stakes for the writer and the executive?

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:09.630
Carole Kirschner: Well for the writer the
stakes are kind of obvious. It’s this is

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:17.850
either going to launch or advance my career.
Or, this is something I love so much I can’t

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.390
wait to do this. I want them to buy it so
that I can do it. And as a side note, if you

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:28.890
love it that much, write it yourself. But
that’s a side topic…&nbsp;So what is at stake

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:36.320
for the writer is, will this help my career?
Now that is the same thing that is at stake

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.260
for the executive, because the executive is
dying to find a great project that is going

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.790
to make them look good….because in Hollywood
everybody wants to look good. It’s about

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:53.879
looking good. So if you’re picking the winning
horse you are looking good. The executive

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:59.140
you are pitching it to most likely has to
go pitch it to their boss. So you want to

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:04.739
make sure you give the person you are pitching
to a logline that they can then take to their

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:10.249
boss and as an easy way to pitch your project
because they become your advocate and they

00:01:10.249 --> 00:01:15.770
want to look good in front of the boss. So
they are hoping that your pitch is going to

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.450
make them look good. Film Courage: O.k. So
let’s say in this hypothetical meeting they

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:24.499
don’t want the script that you’re pitching
but they like you, there is a sensibility

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:32.061
about you. They like your style but your foot
is kind of in the door, but not totally. They

00:01:32.061 --> 00:01:36.969
say “Hey, this isn’t what we’re looking
for….” What would normally be said and

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:38.590
done in this situation? Carole Kirschner:
What would normally be said is “Come back

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:44.069
with other ideas.” So you would come in
and pitch other ideas. Or they might say if

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:49.381
they really like you “We have a project
that we think you’d be great for…” So

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:56.289
they’re pitching you a project. If they
really liked you or if they didn’t respond

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:00.509
or want to use that material, but they wanted
to be in business with you, they would be

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:07.670
in business with you. Film Courage: So if
they say come back to us with something else.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:12.019
You come back home, put your keys down after
the meeting, how soon are you working on this

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:16.400
next thing, knowing that there is a person
who may choose something I do and has the

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.430
ability to launch me? How fast am I getting
this script to this person? How urgently am

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:25.459
I contacting them? Carole Kirschner: That
is a great question. You want to stay on their

00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:29.710
radar, that is really important. You don’t
want six months to go by. But you want to

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:36.060
be prepared for the pitch, so I would say
four weeks. I would say a month to 6 weeks

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.019
at the outset.&nbsp; I would just stay in touch
and say I’m excited and I’m working on

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:46.140
this. Or have your representation say I only
wanted to come back to you with stuff that

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:52.460
is fully formed or that is ready to pitch.
Now if they say “Just come back with some

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:59.049
ideas,” I wouldn’t wait more than two
or three weeks. I would come back. I would

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:04.999
use all of your time, all of your energy,
all of your talent to come up with great ideas

00:03:04.999 --> 00:03:10.640
that have a beginning, middle and an end and
compelling characters that you can talk about.&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.849
Film Courage: How much is too eager, though?
Because you know, someone could be living

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.459
in 100-degree weather in the San Fernando
Valley with no air conditioning and they came

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:24.829
out to Los Angeles so excited and they feel
like this is their chance! But the mere fact

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:31.330
that they are so, so ready, repels…Carole
Kirschner: Yes! You don’t want to be desperate.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:40.579
People smell desperation. So if you came back
the next day, that would be too soon. I wouldn’t

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:47.079
even do it the next week. I would wait a minimum
of two weeks. Because if you come back the

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:54.209
next day then they will think that you didn’t
put time into it. On the other hand, in this

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:57.849
business the way it works is you wait, you
wait, you wait and then you get a call “We

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:02.579
need it now! We want you to come in tomorrow.”
That actually happens. And you have to be

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:07.749
prepared for that because suddenly something
shifted on the other side of the table and

00:04:07.749 --> 00:04:13.200
they need you and they need it right away
so be prepared to do that. But don’t be

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.209
the person who says “I can come back in
tomorrow?” Film Courage: Sounds like three

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:26.389
weeks is a safe zone. I like that. Carole
Kirschner: It is…..get the meeting on the

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:33.050
books though…. Film Courage: Are you doing
this right away while you are there? Carole

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.610
Kirschner: No, no. That would be in the next
couple of days, you would say “I’m going

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:42.710
to be ready to come back in. I’ll let you
know. It will probably be within three weeks.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:46.090
Should I make the appointment now or later?”
and you talk to their assistant. QUESTION:

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:46.460
Anything you can add about the follow-up pitch
meeting?

